{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent returns are negative.",
    "Trend-lag / underreaction to regime breaks: after a peak, the model decays too slowly and keeps projecting mid/high-70s while the market sells off into the 60s. Increase sensitivity to recent momentum (e.g., if last 5–10 day slope is negative, steepen the forecast decline).",
    "Horizon-dependent error: bias grows with horizon (smallest in days 1–5, largest in days ~10–30). Use a horizon-scaled correction (larger downward adjustment and/or steeper negative drift for longer horizons).",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: following a short-term bounce in history, the model extrapolates a recovery (often back toward 75–85) that does not materialize; realized prices continue mean-reverting lower. Cap upside and enforce mean reversion toward a lower anchor when the broader 20–30d trend is down.",
    "Volatility/level mismatch: the model’s forecast range is too narrow around a too-high mean; realized path shows sustained lower levels rather than oscillation around the model mean. Re-center forecasts to a rolling realized mean (e.g., last 10–20d) and reduce any upward drift unless confirmed by multiple consecutive positive closes."
  ]
}
